Day 23 – Rate Limiting (Token Bucket) ⛔

Rate limiting is a core concept in backend systems to control how many requests a client or user can make in a given time window. The Token Bucket algorithm is one of the most popular implementations.

1️⃣ What is Rate Limiting?

Rate limiting is used to:

Prevent DDoS attacks

Protect APIs / microservices

Ensure fair usage among users

Example:

Limit = 100 requests per minute per user

If a user exceeds this, their requests are rejected or delayed.
Rate limiting is a technique used in software systems to control the number of requests a user, client, or system can make to an API or service within a specified time period.

It ensures fair usage, protects resources, and prevents abuse or overload.

Key Points

Purpose of Rate Limiting

Prevent DDoS attacks or spamming

Protect server resources (CPU, memory, DB)

Ensure fair usage among users

Maintain system stability and performance

How It Works

Each user/client is allowed N requests per time unit (e.g., 100 requests/minute).

Requests exceeding the limit are rejected or delayed.

Common Types of Rate Limiting
Type	Description
Fixed Window	Limit requests per fixed time window (e.g., 100 req/min).
Sliding Window	Limit requests based on a rolling time window for smoother control.
Token Bucket	Allows bursts, refills tokens at a constant rate (most flexible).
Leaky Bucket	Smooths traffic, no bursts allowed, processes requests at constant rate.
Example
Limit: 5 requests per second

Scenario:
- User sends 3 requests → Allowed
- User sends 4th request in the same second → Rejected
- Next second, counter resets → Requests allowed again

Use Cases in Real Systems:

API gateways

Banking transactions

Login attempts (e.g., 5 failed logins/minute)

Streaming services (limit concurrent streams per user)

Interview-Friendly Answer

Rate limiting is a technique to restrict the number of requests a client can make to a system in a given period.
 It protects resources, ensures fair usage, and prevents system overload.
 Common implementations include fixed window, sliding window, token bucket, and leaky bucket algorithms.

2️⃣ Token Bucket Algorithm – Concept 🎯

The Token Bucket algorithm works like this:

There is a “bucket” that holds tokens.

Tokens are added to the bucket at a fixed rate.

Each request consumes a token.

If the bucket has no tokens, the request is rejected or queued.

Key properties:

Allows bursts of requests (up to bucket capacity)

Smooths traffic over time

Efficient for APIs / services

✅ Illustration
Bucket capacity = 5 tokens
Token refill rate = 1 token/sec

Time 0: bucket full → 5 requests allowed
Time 1s: 1 token added → 1 request allowed
Time 2s: 1 token added → 1 request allowed
...

Burst handling: sudden 5 requests allowed

Rate control: cannot exceed refill rate in long run

3️⃣ Simple Java Implementation
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class TokenBucket {

    private final int capacity;
    private final int refillRate; // tokens per second
    private AtomicInteger tokens;
    private long lastRefillTimestamp;

    public TokenBucket(int capacity, int refillRate) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.refillRate = refillRate;
        this.tokens = new AtomicInteger(capacity);
        this.lastRefillTimestamp = System.currentTimeMillis();
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
        refill();

        if (tokens.get() > 0) {
            tokens.decrementAndGet();
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    private void refill() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        long secondsSinceLast = (now - lastRefillTimestamp) / 1000;
        int refillTokens = (int) (secondsSinceLast * refillRate);

        if (refillTokens > 0) {
            tokens.set(Math.min(capacity, tokens.get() + refillTokens));
            lastRefillTimestamp = now;
        }
    }
}
Usage
TokenBucket bucket = new TokenBucket(5, 1); // capacity=5, refill 1/sec

if(bucket.allowRequest()) {
    System.out.println("Request allowed");
} else {
    System.out.println("Request denied");
}
